--- body ---
## Context

Phase M2.3 reads the about 115 held one-off documents not yet extracted
(English, Indonesian, Vietnamese, French; PDF with tables, HTML,
spreadsheets, one scan; the largest text layer about 950k characters).
The accepted specification v1 (tag `jetp-spec-v1`) fixes a default design,
two blind local readers of different makers plus a hosted arbiter
(`docs/jetp-extraction.md` § 6.3, § 6.4, § 12, § 14;
`docs/jetp-operation.md` § 5 to 7), and already allows a hosted pair of
different makers (operation § 5). The author's decisions of 2026-10-01
(log) put the design itself up for comparison before any reader is
selected ([[1873]]): output bound to the ontology, no black-box commercial
engine, architectures compared and not only models.

Binding constraints, from the spec: no item queued for the author, the
held-out reference answers the only human check; every proposal carries a
verbatim quote whose locator resolves; readers calibrated on held-out
reference answers; a method version pins model, prompt, weights and
serving; hosted calls under zero retention and `local_only` documents on
padme (RTX A4000 16 GB, RTX 3060 12 GB); a budget of the order of the
earlier spike (USD 14 to 26 for models in total); no heavy dependency
without justification.

The survey is `conception/jetp-m2-3-reading-design-survey-2026-10-01.md`.
It also counts the reference lines per stratum and records that their
provenance (a person's reading, blind to any machine) is not derivable from
the ledger for any family; the pilot protocol must state which lines it
treats as reference answers.

Dependencies: [[1873]] (selection and calibration) waits for the chosen
design. The reading protocol of [[1509]] is written for the default design;
if the pilot picks another, 1509 is revised in the same method version.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The survey report is merged (`conception/jetp-m2-3-reading-design-survey-2026-10-01.md`)
- [ ] Reference-line counts per stratum (language × statement shape, and country) are recorded, with the strata under 30 held-out items named
- [ ] A pre-registered pilot protocol is committed before any pilot call: the candidate designs; the metrics (precision, recall, agree-but-wrong rate, locator-resolution rate, ontology-conformance rate, cost, operational complexity); a tuning-part sample stratified by country × format × statement shape, with its size stated; and the decision rule
- [ ] The pilot is run under the budgets of [[1874]], and its results pass a quality check by a non-author checker (cross-model or second vendor), findings recorded here (pilot rule, [[1500]])
- [ ] The chosen design is recorded as a method version; or, when the winner departs from the accepted spec (for example a single reader plus a checker breaks the two-readers rule of extraction § 6.3), a spec amendment is proposed to the author with the pilot evidence
- [ ] The protocol carries the Gemini 3.8 Flash arm, the batch-pricing rule and the zero-retention check of batch endpoints (section below), run before any pilot call

## Gemini 3.8 Flash and batch pricing (2026-10-01)

Author decisions of 2026-10-01 (see log). Pilot protocol items:

- Gemini 3.8 Flash (`google/gemini-3.8-flash`) is a candidate arbiter and a
  candidate transcription reader (operation § 5: the two hosted roles). It
  is not a main reader: readers are open-weight (operation § 5), and making
  it one needs a spec amendment.
- Maker constraint, as the spec reads: "different families" and "different
  makers" bind the two readers only (operation § 5: "The two readers are
  from different makers" and "Vendor means LLM family, not billing
  channel"; extraction § 6.3 and § 6.4: two readers, or two transcription
  readers, of different families). No sentence requires the arbiter to
  differ from a reader's maker. So Gemma 4 (Google) as a reader would not
  by itself bar Gemini as arbiter. It would weaken the arbiter's
  independence (correlated same-maker errors), so the pilot records the
  agree-but-wrong rate for that pairing. Operation § 7 caps paid spend per
  vendor per month (USD 80, "the arbiter's maker"), which counts Google
  once across roles. Two transcription readers must differ in family, so
  Gemini can be only one of them.
- Retirement risk: a closed model that is retired or repriced forces a
  replacement, which is a new method version with new controls and a new
  calibration on the held-out reference answers (extraction § 6.3,
  operation § 5). Open-weight models pinned on padme do not carry it.
- Latency is not a constraint for M2.3 runs. Every hosted candidate is
  priced at batch rates where a batch endpoint exists (OpenRouter `:batch`
  variants, provider batch APIs).
- Verification item, run before any pilot call: confirm that each batch
  endpoint honours the zero-retention requirement (operation § 5: every
  hosted call fails closed without it), by the request setting and by the
  endpoint listing, and record the endpoint that served each call. A batch
  endpoint that does not honour it is not used and its batch price does
  not apply; the candidate is priced at the standard rate.

Gemini arm cost (derived, not measured). Basis: the 15 documents drawn by
[[1895]], 2,567,739 characters of text layer (HTML documents counted as raw
markup, so an overstatement). Prices checked 2026-10-01: USD 0.75 in / 3.75
out per M tokens; batch is half: USD 0.375 in / 1.875 out.

- Document tokens: 2.568M / 4 = 0.64M; 2.568M / 3 = 0.86M.
- Input only, documents alone, at batch: 0.64 x 0.375 = USD 0.24; 0.86 x
  0.375 = USD 0.32.
- With the per-member token scenarios of 1895 (input 0.7 / 1.1 / 1.8M,
  output 0.25 / 0.5 / 1.5M) at batch: 0.7 x 0.375 + 0.25 x 1.875 = USD
  0.73; 1.1 x 0.375 + 0.5 x 1.875 = USD 1.35; 1.8 x 0.375 + 1.5 x 1.875 =
  USD 3.49.
- The same scenarios at the standard rate: USD 1.46 / 2.70 / 6.98.

So the Gemini arm costs about USD 0.7 to 3.5 at batch, central about USD
1.35, if batch honours zero retention, and about USD 1.5 to 7.0 otherwise.
As an arbiter it sees only escalated items, so its input is smaller than a
full read. The survey's arbiter cost (USD 5 to 55) is at non-batch rates and
is to be repriced at batch rates in the protocol.
